.TH JPI 5 "September 29, 2026" "JPI2Excel 2.0.0" "File Formats"
.SH NAME
jpi \- JP Instruments engine-monitor download format
.SH DESCRIPTION
Files conventionally named with a
.B .JPI
extension contain engine-monitor configuration and compressed flight records.
This page describes the legacy EDM-700/800 family supported by JPI2Excel,
based on the project's fixtures and reverse-engineered JPI-Parser material.
It is not an official JP Instruments binary-format specification.
.PP
The three current fixtures record model code 700 and software value 306.
One is known to originate from an EDM-800.
The recorded model code is authoritative and is not rewritten to match
an externally reported instrument model.
.PP
Later JPI formats differ and may include protocol records, GPS channels,
configuration XML, and version trailers.
Do not apply the legacy framing below unconditionally to other layouts.
JPI2Excel also supports the EDM-730 protocol-2 layout described under
.BR "PROTOCOL-2 EDM-730 LAYOUT" .
.SH LEGACY FILE LAYOUT
.nf
ASCII dollar-record header, ending with $L and CRLF
binary area of exactly ($L value * 256) bytes
    declared flight allocations from $D entries
    unused space in the final physical block
$E,4*5D followed by CRLF
end of file
.fi
.PP
Let
.I data_start
be the zero-based byte offset immediately after the CRLF ending the
.B $L
record.
All end offsets in this page are exclusive.
The current legacy fixtures have no bytes after the end marker.
.SH ASCII RECORDS
Each header record has the form:
.PP
.nf
$tag,field1,field2,...*HH\er\en
.fi
.PP
Here
.B HH
is a two-digit hexadecimal checksum.
XOR all bytes between the leading dollar sign and the asterisk, excluding
both delimiters, and compare with the checksum.
CRLF terminates the record.
Spaces in numeric fields are significant to the checksum even though
numeric conversion may ignore them.
.PP
The supported legacy header requires
.BR $U ,
.BR $A ,
.BR $F ,
.BR $T ,
.BR $C ,
one or more
.B $D
entries, and a terminating
.B $L
record.
.SS "$U: identifier"
Aircraft, device, or user identification text.
It can contain an aircraft registration.
Preserve it as recorded.
.SS "$A: alarm settings"
The eight legacy numeric fields are interpreted in this order:
.PP
.nf
Field  Meaning                         Example
1      High battery voltage, tenths V  305 = 30.5 V
2      Low battery voltage, tenths V   240 = 24.0 V
3      High EGT differential (DIF)     500
4      High CHT                       400
5      Cooling-rate magnitude (CLD)   200
6      High TIT                       1700
7      High oil temperature           220
8      Low oil temperature             95
.fi
.PP
The example temperature thresholds are Fahrenheit; the cooling-rate threshold
has the semantic value \-200 degrees per minute.
Use file-specific units when interpreting a download.
The eight-field layout does not establish MAP, fuel, or oil-pressure alarm
thresholds; absent thresholds must not be supplied from assumed defaults.
.SS "$F: fuel configuration"
The first field selects fuel units.
Code 0 denotes U.S. gallons, with gallons per hour for fuel flow, in the
supported family.
Other fields are preserved as raw configuration.
Current JPI2Excel decoding rejects unvalidated fuel-unit codes.
.SS "$T: download timestamp"
Six fields give month, day, two-digit year, hour, minute, and packed
seconds/milliseconds.
For final field
.IR n :
.PP
.nf
seconds      = n // 1000
milliseconds = n % 1000
.fi
.PP
Years below 75 map to 2000 through 2074; 75 through 99 map to 1975 through 1999
in the current decoder.
For example, the fields
.B 9,19,26,20,48,4795
represent 2026-09-19 20:48:04.795.
This is the download time, not a substitute for a flight start time.
No timezone is invented.
.SS "$C: system configuration"
Carries model code, configuration words, and software information.
The current legacy layout has five numeric fields, for example:
.PP
.nf
$C, 700,63741,32689, 1556, 306*4B
.fi
.PP
Configuration bits affect units and enabled sensors.
Engine-temperature and outside-air-temperature units can differ.
The recorded model code and software value also select binary checksum behavior.
.SS "$D: flight directory"
Each entry describes one flight allocation:
.PP
.nf
$D,flight_id,halfsize*HH
flight_block_bytes = halfsize * 2
.fi
.PP
The size is in 16-bit words.
Each allocation contains its flight header, compressed records, and possibly
one final byte of word-alignment padding.
Flight allocations occur in directory order.
Duplicate flight IDs within one file are rejected by the current parser;
IDs repeated across separate input files are preserved.
.SS "$L: physical block count"
Terminates the ASCII header.
For the validated legacy family, its numeric value counts physical 256-byte
blocks, not flights, bytes, or samples.
.SS "$E: end marker"
The current legacy end marker is:
.PP
.nf
$E,4*5D\er\en
.fi
.PP
It starts at the physical end calculated from
.BR $L .
The numeric 4 is not used as a flight count.
Its ASCII checksum is validated independently.
.SH LOGICAL AND PHYSICAL BOUNDARIES
Calculate:
.PP
.nf
logical_length  = 2 * sum(all $D halfsize values)
logical_end     = data_start + logical_length
block_count     = $L value
physical_length = block_count * 256
physical_end    = data_start + physical_length
slack_length    = physical_end - logical_end
.fi
.PP
The supported legacy framing requires:
.PP
.nf
block_count == (logical_length + 255) // 256
$E starts at physical_end
0 <= slack_length < 256
.fi
.PP
Decode flights only within their directory allocations, ending at
.IR logical_end .
Do not scan forward for a replacement end marker if the marker is missing
at the expected physical boundary.
.SS "Final-block slack"
The bytes in
.B [logical_end, physical_end)
belong to no declared flight.
They may be nonzero and may contain fragments or complete records with valid
checksums.
Their contents do not create additional samples and are not subject to
flight-record checksum validation.
.PP
These bytes are distinct from a possible one-byte pad inside a
.B $D
allocation.
The former fill out the final physical block; the latter is included in a
flight's word-counted allocation.
.PP
Record-shaped slack is consistent with stale contents in a reused storage
or download buffer.
That explanation is an inference; the exact mechanism and original source
of the bytes remain unknown.
A checksum-valid orphan record lacks the preceding delta state needed to
assign reliable sensor values or timestamps.
.SS "Fixture evidence"
The following decimal offsets and lengths are measured from the original files:
.PP
.nf
File          data_start  logical bytes  $L   $E offset  slack
U260731.JPI          194          78904  309       79298    200
U260828.JPI          308         129340  506      129844    196
U260919.JPI          232          94240  369       94696    224
.fi
.PP
All three satisfy the block-count and physical-end relationships exactly.
.SH BINARY FLIGHTS
The supported legacy flight header is 15 bytes.
Multi-byte header words are big-endian.
.PP
.nf
Offset  Size  Field
0       2     Flight ID
2       2     First sensor/configuration word
4       2     Second sensor/configuration word
6       1     Fuel-unit code
7       1     Rated horsepower setting
8       2     Recording interval in seconds
10      2     Packed flight date
12      2     Packed flight time
14      1     Header checksum
.fi
.PP
Sensor-enable bits identify monitored EGT, CHT, TIT, and other channels.
Counts derived from these bits describe recorded channels; TIT count alone
does not establish the number of physical turbochargers.
The packed date/time is decoded using the legacy rules of the pinned decoder.
Midnight is valid; invalid timestamps must not be replaced with download time.
.PP
Compressed records are variable length.
In the legacy eight-bit layout, a record begins with two matching mask bytes
and a repeat-count byte, followed by mask-selected control bytes, sign bytes,
delta magnitudes, and a checksum byte.
The masks and controls determine the record length.
.PP
Sensor values are reconstructed by adding signed deltas to accumulated state
and applying channel-specific scaling.
Unchanged initialized channels retain their values.
Repeated samples replay the previous values before the new record is applied.
The recording interval determines elapsed seconds; MARK transitions can select
one-second fast recording and restore the configured interval.
.PP
Do not interpret compressed bytes as fixed-width table rows or decode an
isolated record without its preceding state.
.SH BINARY CHECKSUMS
For legacy model 700 with software at least 296, or model 800 with software
at least 300, the decoder uses an additive modulo-256 checksum.
The sum of the entire header or complete record, including its checksum byte,
must be zero modulo 256.
Older supported branches use XOR, which must also produce zero over the
complete header or record.
.PP
The current fixtures use the additive branch.
Both header and record checksums remain mandatory inside declared flights.
Neither the one-byte word pad nor final-block slack is a separate
checksummed flight record.
.SH UNITS AND OPTIONAL CHANNELS
Units come from file configuration and channel-specific rules.
Examples include manifold pressure in tenths of inches of mercury, battery
voltage in tenths of volts, and U.S.-gallon fuel flow in tenths of
gallons per hour.
Engine and outside-air temperature units need not match.
.PP
Later layouts can contain latitude, longitude, altitude, and speed, often
identified as LAT, LNG, ALT, and SPD.
They must not be synthesized when absent.
Twin-capable formats use engine-specific channels on a shared flight timeline.
Apart from the EDM-730, later and multi-engine layouts are outside current
decoding support.
.SH PROTOCOL-2 EDM-730 LAYOUT
.nf
$U, $A, $F, $T, $C (nine fields), $P,2, $H, $D..., $L, CRLF
flights packed back to back by actual byte length
$E,4*5D CRLF
JPI followed by XOR-0x02-encoded configuration XML
$E,4*5D CRLF
$V,Created by EDM730 ... CRLF
end of file
.fi
.PP
Each
.B $D
size is the flight's byte length divided by two, rounded up; a flight of odd
length ends one byte before its allocation and the next flight starts there.
.B $L
still counts 256-byte blocks of the
.B $D
allocations, but the first
.B $E
follows the last flight directly.
A
.B $D
size of zero lists no flight.
.PP
Flight headers are 29 bytes and carry a starting latitude and longitude.
Records use 16-bit masks and additive checksums.
Positions are in units of 1/6000 degree and are exported as decimal degrees.
The configuration XML contains the tail number; JPI2Excel does not export it.
It reads only the
.B carbureted
attribute of the
.B EDM
element: when it is 1, the CDT channel is carburetor temperature and is
exported as CRB.
.SH VALIDATION
JPI2Excel rejects malformed required metadata, inconsistent block counts,
a missing or misplaced end marker, invalid flight boundaries, and bad
ASCII or binary checksums.
Nonzero slack that satisfies the physical framing is accepted.
Only declared flight samples are exported.
.PP
The 256-byte rule is validated from the supplied legacy family.
Its applicability to all other firmware versions, models, or later protocols
has not been established.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B testdata/*.JPI
